## The writing

Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish a reply where a recognised json code fence contains two JSON objects followed by a later top-level object from a reply that contains only that later top-level object. The documented rule states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply A is the text: '```json\n{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}\n```\n{"c": 3}'. Reply B is the text: '{"c": 3}'. The rule requires Reply A to be scored as {"b": 2}, because the fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {"b": 2}; it requires Reply B to be scored as {"c": 3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {"c": 3}. The check, however, treats the entire fence content as a single candidate, which fails to parse as one JSON object, so it skips the fenced pool and falls back to the top-level scan. That scan finds all three objects in Reply A and returns the last top-level object, {"c": 3}, the same answer it gives for Reply B. I would be wrong if the check actually returns {"b": 2} for Reply A, or if the rule is interpreted to mean that a fence containing multiple objects does not count as holding any single object and thus the top-level fallback is correct.

